		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=4922&amp;p=35992#p35992"><![CDATA[
Hello.<br><br><br>I’ve been manually saving repeaters to my GD77 while driving around, checking availability via RepeaterBook. Over time, this has resulted in an unstructured and chaotic list of stored repeaters, making navigation increasingly difficult.  <br><br>I recently discovered that the CPS software supports importing and exporting channel data in CSV format. Since I’m quite proficient with Excel and LibreOffice Calc, this seems like a good way to properly organize my channels. Additionally, as I am blind, I find the built-in CPS organizational functions somewhat cumbersome to use.  <br><br>My goals are:  <br>- Creating zones  <br>- Moving channels into zones  <br>- Changing the order of channels  <br><br>Before I proceed, I have some specific questions regarding the CSV workflow:  <br>- What should I keep in mind when working with the exported CSV files?  <br>- What delimiter does CPS use—comma, semicolon, or tab (TSV format)?  <br>- Are frequency values stored with a decimal point or comma? In Germany, we use a comma as the decimal separator and a period as a thousands separator, which could cause issues.  <br>- When editing the CSV, can I assign a channel to a zone that does not yet exist in CPS, or must I first create the zone within CPS before referencing it in the file?  <br><br>Any insights or best practices would be greatly appreciated!<br><br>73s,<br>Richard, DO9RE<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=8178">DO9RE</a> — Fri Feb 28, 2025 12:01 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
